<section id="asks" class="part">
<h2>What this version holds</h2>
<p>You commented on version 4 this morning. This version applies your comments. Each item is one block, which holds:</p>
<ul>
<li>its heading, with the rule's number and title, and the item's state;</li>
<li>its new text whole, with the added words underlined;</li>
<li>its old text, collapsed: tap its label to open it;</li>
<li>what it changes and why;</li>
<li>your comment of today, quoted, and the answer.</li>
</ul>
<p>The Select button beside a block's heading selects all of that. Only the references at the block's bottom stay outside its range.</p>
<p>Each item has one of four states:</p>
<ul>
<li>Accepted, lands now: you said LGTM. Seven items: rules 11, 13, 15 and 22, and three fixes. Rule 22 lands with the one change that you asked for.</li>
<li>Redrafted, waits for your read: you objected, and the new text is drafted from your words, as a goal or a principle with its reason, not as steps. Nine items. Rule 2 also waits for a fresh coordinator's or a Fable judgement, as you asked.</li>
<li>Waits for your read: the fixes in the parts that you did not reach, shown in this form. Ten items.</li>
<li>Dropped: two fixes. The cold read's sentence on loading <code>delegating.md</code> stood in three places, and it now stands in one.</li>
</ul>
<p>Nothing that you objected to lands. The cold reader read the coordinator skill as a worker would, not as the coordinating session. So several fixes misread a rule's intent, and those are redrafted from your words or dropped.</p>
<ul>
<li>Times are UTC. Your clock is one hour ahead of UTC.</li>
<li>In a quote of your words, “…” marks a cut, and [?] follows a word that the dictation may have heard wrong.</li>
</ul>
<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span>
<ul>
