<section id="rules" class="part">
<h2>The nine rules, regrounded</h2>
<p>These are the nine rules that you sent back on 1 November. A worker grounded each one again in your words at the time, and version 4 showed its texts. You accepted rules 11, 13, 15 and 22. Rules 2, 4, 12, 17 and 20 are redrafted from your comments of today. Rules 18, 19 and 21 fold into rule 17.</p>
<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span>
<ul>
<li>The note <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress/blob/main/explorations/coordinator/process-engineering/skill-rules-regrounded-2026-11-01.md"><code>skill-rules-regrounded-2026-11-01.md</code></a>, commit <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress/commit/052c96dcd87d532d3b14539b60076f9d9a4173b2"><code>052c96dcd</code></a>, which quotes your words at the time for each rule; <a href="https://github.com/palimondo/fortress/blob/main/explorations/coordinator/HELD.md">HELD.md</a>, “Held from his page comments (2026-11-01)”, 22:19 to 23:25.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section id="r2" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Rule 2 &middot; SKILL.md, “The standing goal”, a new last bullet &middot; redrafted &middot; waits for a fresh coordinator's or a Fable judgement</span>Restate the goal before each step</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text: a new bullet</span>
<ul><li>Before each ask, proposal or launch, state the project's goals and principles again to yourself, in a few words, and weigh the step's competing aims by them: what the step is for, what it must bring about and what it gives up. Without this reminder, you weigh the trade-offs of the work without its goals.</li></ul>
</div></div>
<details class="prior"><summary>Version 4's text (tap to open)</summary><div>
<ul><li>Before each ask, proposal or launch, reground the step in the goal: what it is for, what it must bring about, how the aspects that compete weigh, and whether its premise is checked or only assumed. <span class="cut">Give the curator these lines with each ask or proposal, so that the curator can correct a wrong premise before the work runs. Leave out the mission, which this skill states.</span></li></ul>
</div></details>
<div class="note"><p><strong>What it changes and why.</strong> Today no rule makes the coordinator weigh a step against the project's goals before it acts. With this bullet, the coordinator states the goals and principles to itself again before each ask, proposal or launch, and weighs the step's competing aims by them. Version 4's last two sentences go: the lines are a reminder for the coordinator, not lines for you, and the goals are what the reminder restates.</p></div>
<p><strong>Your comment.</strong></p>
<blockquote class="held"><p>“This is a good improvement, but I don't see how the new text achieves my goal. And especially don't like or understand the desired effect of the last sentence, ‘leave out the mission, which this skill states’. … It's a self-prompt, basically, which I hope will lead you to periodically re-evaluate the weighing of competing goals, so that you order them from the project goals and project principles against each other. So this is not yet the expression that I would sign off on. … I believe a fresher coordinator would be better suited for the job, not you right now.”</p></blockquote>
<p><strong>The answer.</strong> You're right on both points. Version 4 aimed the lines at you, as a check that you could correct, and its last sentence cut the very goals that the lines are to restate. This text makes it a reminder that the coordinator gives itself, to weigh each step by the goals and principles. It is a worker's draft: it lands only after the fresh coordinator that boots after the compaction, or a Fable judgement, has judged it.</p>
<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span>
<ul>
<li>Your comment of today: 05:59, on version 4's new text.</li>
<li>Your words at the time: 28 October, 12:13, 12:18 and 12:24; your page comment of 1 November, 22:19. The note, its section on rule 2.</li>
<li>HELD.md, “Held in chat (2026-11-02)”, the line of 07:01 to 07:34.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section id="r4" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Rule 4 &middot; SKILL.md, “Talking to the curator”, a new last bullet under “While the curator reads turn by turn” &middot; redrafted &middot; waits for your read</span>Small turns for page comments</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text: a new bullet</span>
<ul><li>While the curator comments on a page, the page keeps the comments, so each comment's turn stays small: “.” in chat, and the point kept in your context. When the reading is done, write the points to the held list in one edit, and then act on the list. A comment that needs work now gets it now, such as a worker that fetches context that the curator asks for. A comment that opens a discussion gets its answer on its thread.</li></ul>
</div></div>
<details class="prior"><summary>Version 4's text (tap to open)</summary><div>
<ul><li>When the curator comments on a page, the page's threads hold the points. <span class="cut">Reply on each thread with its held line and “holding”,</span> reply “.” in chat, and write the held lines of the whole reading in one edit and one commit when the reading is done.</li></ul>
</div></details>
<div class="note"><p><strong>What it changes and why.</strong> Today the skill has no rule for a page's comments, and on 29 October the coordinator wrote each point three times and committed the held list after each comment. With this bullet, a comment costs a “.” in chat, the held list gets one edit when you finish, and then the coordinator acts on the list. “Holding” on each thread goes, and the bullet names the two cases that get an answer at once: work that a comment needs now, and a discussion that you open.</p></div>
<p><strong>Your comment.</strong></p>
<blockquote class="held"><p>“I'm not interested in the cargo cult of repeating the word holding within each comment. Stated categorically, as it is now, will this prevent you launching workers that need extended context? And I'm also fine with you replying to me in the context, in the comment, if I open a discussion. The decision between whether to just hold my comment for a next rewrite or to do something actionable right now depends on the context. Like, if I say I need more context, I need you to launch the worker and get it in the meantime, right? … You are saying that after the comment, artifact comment tool wakes you up, you must reply something in the main chat, so you are choosing to minimize the traffic by replying just with the dot. That is fine. And that you are gathering the stuff in context, and then you will put it into held lines after I'm done, and then you will act on that list. That makes sense to me. So otherwise, yes.”</p></blockquote>
<p><strong>The answer.</strong> Yes: version 4's text would have held a comment that asks for context, and its worker would have waited for the end of your reading. This bullet launches such a worker at once, while you read on. It drops “holding” on the threads, answers on a thread only in a discussion that you open, and keeps the “.” in chat and the one edit of the held list at the end.</p>
<div class="sources"><span class="label">References</span>
<ul>
<li>Your comment of today: 06:07, on version 4's new text.</li>
<li>Your words at the time: 29 October, 09:14 and 12:35; your page comment of 1 November, 22:27. The note, its section on rule 4; <code>explorations/coordinator/postmortem-2026-10-28/evidence/context-spend.md</code>, the 25 comment turns of 29 October.</li>
</ul>
</div>
</section>

<section id="r11" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Rule 11 &middot; delegating.md, “What a brief holds” &middot; accepted, lands now</span>A short brief</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text, added words underlined</span>
<p class="where-in">A new opening, before the list: every word is new</p>
<p>A brief holds what only you know: the problem, the question, what the answer is for, and where the sources are, the record first. The worker has <code>fortress-repo</code> loaded, and its router loads the parts that the work needs. So leave the method to the worker: give no reading list, reading order or steps, and neither quote nor name a part of <code>fortress-repo</code>. Each costs your context, and the worker chooses its reading well without it.</p>
<p class="where-in">The bullet on entries</p>
<ul><li>The entries that it needs, <span class="add">by number. The worker prints them, and their history where it needs it, with <code>facts-extract.sh</code>.</span> Quote <span class="add">whole</span>, with its limit and its example, <span class="add">only</span> a rule of the curator's <span class="add">that the record does not hold yet</span>.</li></ul>
<p class="where-in">The paragraph after the list</p>
<p>A worker whose task touches the repository loads <code>fortress-repo</code> by itself. That skill tells it which choices are its own. <span class="add">A worker loads no part of this skill by itself. If it does part of your work, such as an analysis, name the parts of this skill that the work needs.</span></p>
</div></div>
<details class="prior"><summary>Old text, on main now (tap to open)</summary><div>
<p class="where-in">The bullet on entries</p>
<ul><li>The entries that it needs, <span class="cut">quoted word for word under their numbers</span>. Quote a rule of the curator's whole, with its limit and its example.</li></ul>
<p class="where-in">The paragraph after the list</p>
