198	<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text: a new bullet</span>
199	<ul><li>Before each ask, proposal or launch, state the project's goals and principles again to yourself, in a few words, and weigh the step's competing aims by them: what the step is for, what it must bring about and what it gives up. Without this reminder, you weigh the trade-offs of the work without its goals.</li></ul>
200	</div></div>
201	<details class="prior"><summary>Version 4's text (tap to open)</summary><div>
202	<ul><li>Before each ask, proposal or launch, reground the step in the goal: what it is for, what it must bring about, how the aspects that compete weigh, and whether its premise is checked or only assumed. <span class="cut">Give the curator these lines with each ask or proposal, so that the curator can correct a wrong premise before the work runs. Leave out the mission, which this skill states.</span></li></ul>
203	</div></details>
204	<div class="note"><p><strong>What it changes and why.</strong> Today no rule makes the coordinator weigh a step against the project's goals before it acts. With this bullet, the coordinator states the goals and principles to itself again before each ask, proposal or launch, and weighs the step's competing aims by them. Version 4's last two sentences go: the lines are a reminder for the coordinator, not lines for you, and the goals are what the reminder restates.</p></div>
205	<p><strong>Your comment.</strong></p>
206	<blockquote class="held"><p>“This is a good improvement, but I don't see how the new text achieves my goal. And especially don't like or understand the desired effect of the last sentence, ‘leave out the mission, which this skill states’. … It's a self-prompt, basically, which I hope will lead you to periodically re-evaluate the weighing of competing goals, so that you order them from the project goals and project principles against each other. So this is not yet the expression that I would sign off on. … I believe a fresher coordinator would be better suited for the job, not you right now.”</p></blockquote>
207	<p><strong>The answer.</strong> You're right on both points. Version 4 aimed the lines at you, as a check that you could correct, and its last sentence cut the very goals that the lines are to restate. This text makes it a reminder that the coordinator gives itself, to weigh each step by the goals and principles. It is a worker's draft: it lands only after the fresh coordinator that boots after the compaction, or a Fable judgement, has judged it.</p>
